## Enforcement

| Where | How |
|---|---|
| Code | `{{TEST_COMMAND}}` fails the build on a banned synonym |
| Issues / named places | Title prefix vocabulary: {{PREFIXES}} |
| Reports | Reviewed at close — a banned synonym is a finding, not a nit |

**Status:** {{ENFORCEMENT_STATUS}}

**Make the table the source of truth.** Write the gate so it *parses* this
file's banned-synonyms table instead of repeating the list in code. Two copies
of a ban list drift apart, and the copy nobody reads is the one that is wrong.
With the table parsed, adding a row here is what enforces it — no second edit.

**An exemption is a `(path, substring)` pair.** Historical mentions are real:
a rename record has to name the old word, and dated evidence quotes it. Exempt
the *line* that also contains a justifying substring, never the word
everywhere — so the mention stays visible and is justified where it appears,
rather than silently tolerated across the repo.

<!--
If enforcement is not yet automated, say so explicitly and name what would
prove it. An ontology nobody can violate is documentation; an ontology a test
enforces is law. Do not claim the second while shipping the first.
-->

